1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He is said to have the same mother as Hulaqu and Qubilay. He is also said to have been born in Khorasan, Iran  He died in Hulagu's jail. https://fa.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D8%A2%D8%B1%D9%BE%D8%A7%D8%AE%D8%A7%D9%86 اریغ
2 CONC  بوکا یا اریق بوکا (بوقا) است که در برخی از منابع به تحریف، آرتق آمده‌است و او پسر ششم تولی‌خان پسر چنگیزخان است که مادر او مادر  قوبیلای قاآن و هولاکوخان نیز بوده‌است. این اریغ بوکا در تاریخ مغول مشهور است زیرا بر سر منصب خانی بز
2 CONC رگ با قوبیلای قاآن جنگیده و از او شکست خورده و در زندان او جان سپرده‌است.
2 CONT
2 CONT ===
2 CONT Ariq Böke / Arigbugda Khan / Erik Böge / Arig Böke (died c.a.1266),  was yongest son of Tolui (1193 Mongolia - 1232 Mongolia). (Wikipea).
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT * [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ariq_B%C3%B6ke Wikipedia]
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Ariq Böke (after 1219–1266), the components of his name also spelled Arigh, Arik and Bukha, Buka (Mongolian: Аригбөх; Chinese: 阿里不哥), was the seventh and youngest son of Tolui (1192–1232), a son of Genghis Khan. After the death of
2 CONC  his brother the Great Khan Möngke, Ariq Böke claimed the title of the Great Khan of the Mongol Empire and briefly took power while his brothers Kublai (more commonly known as Kublai Khan) and Hulagu (more commonly known as Hulagu
2 CONC  Khan) were absent from Mongolia homeland. When Kublai returned for an election in 1260, rival factions could not agree, and elected simultaneous claimants Kublai and Ariq Böke to the throne, resulting in the Toluid Civil War that
2 CONC  fragmented the Mongol Empire. Ariq Böke was supported by the traditionalists of the Mongol Empire, while his brother Kublai Khan was supported by the senior princes of North China and Manchuria.
2 CONT
2 CONT ------------------
2 CONT The story of Qubilãy Qããn and his younger brother, Arigh Bokã: Arigh 
2 CONT Bokã had contested Qubilãy’s election as Great Khãn on grounds that the election 
2 CONT gathering (quriltãy) should have been held on the sacred land of Mogholestãn (Mongolia) 
2 CONT and not in China. He subsequently convened a quriltãy in Mogholestãn which elected him 
2 CONT as Great Khãn. War broke out between the two brothers and after several years of struggle, 
2 CONT Arigh Bokã surrendered in 662/1264. According to Persian sources, Qubilãy castigated 
2 CONT him for his treachery and, on the advice of his "Chinese counselors," ordered a peculiar 
2 CONT punishment for his rebellious brother: Arigh Bokã was incarcerated in a prison with walls made of cactus spines ( khãr-e moghilãn ), where he perished within a year or two.
2 CONT After a long reign of thirty four years Qubilãy was succeeded by his grandson, 
2 CONT Teymur (Ch’eng-tsung), recognized as the new qããn and nominal suzerain of the il-khãns . (ref: The Saga of Abu-Sa`id Bahâdor Khân, The Abu-saidnâmé
2 CONT By Abolala Soudavar)
