1 OCCU Founder of the Timurid Empire in Persia & Central Asia, (Founder of the Timurid Empire in Persia & Central Asia.), Ruler of Timurid Empire, Amir of the Timurid Empire
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Timur among his other names, commonly known as Tamerlane in the West was a 14th century Turko-Mongol conqueror of much of western and Central Asia, and founder of the Timurid Empire and Timurid dynasty (1370–1405) 
2 CONC in Central Asia, which survived until 1857 as the Mughal Empire of India.
2 CONT
2 CONT Sultan of Turan
2 CONT
2 CONT In his memoirs Timur gave the following information about his ancestry: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "My father told me that we were descendants from Abu-al-Atrak (father of the Turks) the son of Japhet. His fifth son, Aljeh Khan, had twin sons, Tatar and Mogul, who placed their feet on the paths of infidelity. Tumene Khan had a 
2 CONC son Kabul, whose son, Munga Bahadur, was the father of Temugin, called Zengis Khan. Zengis Khan abandoned the duty of a conqueror by slaughtering the people, and plundering the dominions of God, and he put many thousands of Moslem
2 CONC s to death. He bestowed Mawur-ulnaher on his son Zagatai, and appointed my ancestor, Karachar Nevian, to be his minister. "Karacher appointed the plain of Kesh for the residence of the tribe of Berlas (his own tribe), and he subdu
2 CONC ed the countries of Kashgar, Badakshan, and Andecan. He was succeeded by his son Ayettekuz as Sepah Salar (general). Then followed my grandfather, the Ameer Burkul, who retired from office, and contented himself with the governmen
2 CONC t of his own tribe of Berlas. He possessed an incalculable number of sheep and goats, cattle and servants. On his death my father succeeded, but he also preferred seclusion, and the society of learned men."
2 CONT
2 CONT For further information please visit the below link
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timur
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Timur is regarded as a military genius, and as a brilliant tactician with an uncanny ability to work within a highly fluid political structure to win and maintain a loyal following of nomads during his rule in Central Asia. He was
2 CONC  also considered extraordinarily intelligent – not only intuitively but also intellectually. In Samarkand and his many travels, Timur, under the guidance of distinguished scholars, was able to learn the Persian, Mongolian, and Tur
2 CONC kish languages.(according to Ahmad ibn Arabshah, Timur didn't know Arabic) More importantly, Timur was characterized as an opportunist. Taking advantage of his Turco-Mongolian heritage, Timur frequently used either the Islamic rel
2 CONC igion or the law and traditions of the Mongol Empire to achieve his military goals or domestic political aims.Timur, mostly considered a barbarian, in fact was a well learned king, and did enjoy the company of scholars —he was tol
2 CONC erant and generous to them against his nature.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Tamerlan - a Turkish speaking muslim Mongol of the Barla tribe.  
2 CONT
2 CONT Ulugh Beg was the grandson of the conqueror Timur, who is often known as Tamerlane (from Timur-I-Leng meaning Timur the Lame, a title of contempt used by his Persian enemies). Although in this archive we are primarily interested i
2 CONC n Ulugh Beg's achievements in mathematics and astronomy, we need to examine the history of the area since it had such a major impact on Ulugh Beg's life.
2 CONT
2 CONT Timur, Ulugh Beg's grandfather, came from the Turkic Barlas tribe which was a Mongol tribe that was living in Transoxania, today essentially Uzbekistan. He united several Turko-Mongol tribes under his leadership and set out on a c
2 CONC onquest, with his armies of mounted archers, of the area now occupied by Iran, Iraq, and eastern Turkey.
2 CONT
2 CONT Shortly after his grandson Ulugh Beg was born, Timur invaded India and by 1399 he had taken control of Delhi. Timur continued his conquests by extending his empire to the west from 1399 to 1402, winning victories over the Egyptian
2 CONC  Mamluks in Syria and the Ottomans in a battle near Ankara. Timur died in 1405 leading his armies into China.
2 CONT
2 CONT After Timur's death his empire was disputed among his sons. Ulugh Beg's father Shah Rukh was the fourth son of Timur and, by 1407, he had gained overall control of most of the empire, including Iran and Turkistan regaining control
2 CONC  of Samarkand. Samarkand had been the capital of Timur's empire but, although his grandson Ulugh Beg had been brought up at Timur's court, he was seldom in that city. When Timur was not on one of his military campaigns he moved wi
2 CONC th his army from place to place and his court, including his grandson Ulugh Beg, travelled with him.
2 CONT
2 CONT In his memoirs Timur gave the following information about his ancestry: 
2 CONT
2 CONT "My father told me that we were descendants from Abu-al-Atrak (father of the Turks) the son of Japhet. His fifth son, Aljeh Khan, had twin sons, Tatar and Mogul, who placed their feet on the paths of infidelity. Tumene Khan had a 
2 CONC son Kabul, whose son, Munga Bahadur, was the father of Temugin, called Zengis Khan. Zengis Khan abandoned the duty of a conqueror by slaughtering the people, and plundering the dominions of God, and he put many thousands of Moslem
2 CONC s to death. He bestowed Mawur-ulnaher on his son Zagatai, and appointed my ancestor, Karachar Nevian, to be his minister. "Karacher appointed the plain of Kesh for the residence of the tribe of Berlas (his own tribe), and he subdu
2 CONC ed the countries of Kashgar, Badakshan, and Andecan. He was succeeded by his son Ayettekuz as Sepah Salar (general). Then followed my grandfather, the Ameer Burkul, who retired from office, and contented himself with the governmen
2 CONC t of his own tribe of Berlas. He possessed an incalculable number of sheep and goats, cattle and servants. On his death my father succeeded, but he also preferred seclusion, and the society of learned men."
2 CONT
2 CONT p. 198 Iranian History at a Glance http://bit.ly/1Pu8qqA
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT =============
2 CONT
2 CONT On 18th February 1405 AD, the Turco-Mongol conqueror, Amir Timur, died, while on expedition against China after conquering all the lands from the Mediterranean coast of Syria to River Ganges in India, and from the Persian Gulf in 
2 CONC the south to Moscow in the north. He was of ferocious nature and sometimes destroyed entire cities and massacred whole populations. He seized the Chaghatay Khanate of Central Asia-Khorasan, destroyed the Golden Horde of Eurasia, d
2 CONC efeated the Mamluk Empire of Egypt-Syria, conquered the Sultanate of Delhi and shattered the emerging Ottoman Empire, whose sultan, Bayazid I, he captured in battle and took as prisoner to his capital Samarqand. At the same time h
2 CONC e patronized art, architecture and literature, especially the Persian language. He was buried in his capital Samarqand in what is now the Republic of Uzbekistan in the mausoleum known as Gur-e Amir. Born in Kesh, now known as Shah
2 CONC r-e Sabz, 80 km south of the famous Iranian city of Samarqand (presently in Uzbekistan), he had started life as leader of a small band of raiders, and during one such raid, he was shot by arrows that crippled his right leg for lif
2 CONC e; hence his epithet in Persian "Timur-e Lang” (Timur the Lame), which was corrupted by Europeans to Tamerlane. His empire was inherited by his youngest son, Shahrukh, whose mother was an Iranian and who during his long and peacef
2 CONC ul reign did not pursue any policy of expansionism. A century after Timur’s death, his empire was no more as all his descendents vanished from the political scene, except for a great-great grandson, Zaheer od-Din Mohammad Babar, w
2 CONC ho established the Mughal Empire of the Subcontinent that ended in 1857 with the fall of Delhi to the British and the exiling to Burma of the last Timurid ruler, Bahadur Shah Zafar.
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Timur
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Early history
2 CONT
2 CONT Emir Timur feasts in the gardens of Samarkand. Timur was born in Transoxiana , near the City of Kesh (an area now better known as Shahrisabz, "the green city"), some fifty miles south of Samarkand in modern day Uzbekistan, part of
2 CONC  the Chagatai Khanate. His father, Taraqai, a minor noble belonging to the Barlas tribe. The Barlas, a Turko-Mongol tribe which originally were Mongolian tribes that became Turkified and/or became Turkic-speaking or intermingling 
2 CONC with the Turkic peoples. According to Gérard Chaliand, Timur was a Muslim Turk but he saw himself as Genghis Khan's heir.Though not a Chinggisid,he clearly sought to evoke the legacy of Genghis Khan's conquests during his lifetime
2 CONC . At the age of eight or nine, Timur with his mother and brothers, were carried as prisoners to Samarkand by an invading Mongol army. In his childhood, Timur with a small band of followers raided travelers for goods with most bein
2 CONC g animals such as sheep, horses, and cattle. At around 1363, it is believed that Timur tried to steal a sheep from a shepherd but was shot by two arrows; one in his right leg and another in his right hand, where he lost two finger
2 CONC s. Both injuries caused him to be crippled for life. Some believe that Timur suffered his crippling injuries while serving as a mercenary to the khan of Sistan in Khorasan in what is known today as Dasht-i Margo (Desert of Death) 
2 CONC in south-west Afghanistan. Timur's injuries have given him the surname of Timur the Lame or Tamerlane by Europeans. Timur was a Muslim, but while his chief official religious counsellor and advisor was the Hanafi scholar 'Abdu 'l-
2 CONC Jabbar Khwarazmi, his particular persuasion is not known. In Tirmidh, he had come under the influence of his spiritual mentor Sayyid Barakah, a leader from Balkh who is buried alongside Timur in Gur-e Amir.Timur was known to hold 
2 CONC Ali and the Ahlul Bayt in high regard and has been noted by various scholars for his "pro-Alid" stance.[citation needed] Despite this, Timur was noted for attacking Shi’is on Sunni grounds and therefore his own religious inclinati
2 CONC ons remain unclear.
2 CONT
2 CONT Personality
2 CONT
2 CONT Timur is regarded as a military genius and a tactician, with an uncanny ability to work within a highly fluid political structure to win and maintain a loyal following of nomads during his rule in Central Asia. He was also conside
2 CONC red extraordinarily intelligent- not only intuitively but also intellectually. In Samarkand and his many travels, Timur, under the guidance of distinguished scholars was able to learn Persian, Mongolian, and Turkic languages. More
2 CONC  importantly, Timur was characterized as an opportunist. Taking advantage of his Turco-Mongolia heritage, Timur frequently used either the Islamic religion or the law and traditions of the Mongol Empire to achieve his military goa
2 CONC ls or domestic political aims.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/MONGOLS.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.         TERAGAI (-[1360], bur Kesh).  Ahmed ibn Arabshah’s life of Timur (finished in 1435) names “Targai fil Abgai” as the father of Timur[362].  … m  [TEKINA Khatun, daughter of ---] (-[1357]).  
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT a)         '''TIMUR''' (Kesh, near Samarkand, Turkestan [9 Apr] 1336-Otrar 17 Feb 1405, bur Samarkand).  The History of Timur written by Ali of Yezd records the birth of Timur “l’an 736 de l’Hegyre” (Aug 1335/Aug 1336)[373].  He b
2 CONC ecame TIMUR "the Lame" [Ruler] of the Tartars (‘Sultan Kamran Amir Kuth-ud-Din Timur Kurkhan Sahib Keraun’[374]), based at Samarkand.  Ruy Gonzalez de Clavijo records that "Timour Beg is the proper name of that lord, and not Tamer
2 CONC lane as we call him...he became lame on the left side"[375].  … The Memoirs of Timur record the death in 1366 of “Aljay Turkan Agha”[384].  
2 CONT
2 CONT Married: 
2 CONT # ([1357]) ---, daughter of Amir JAKU Berlas. 
2 CONT # ([1357/58]) ALJAZ TURKHAN Aga, daughter of Amir MASHLAH (-1366). 
2 CONT # ([1367]) as her second husband, MULK Khanun, widow of Emir HUSAIN Khan, daughter of KHAZAN Khan.
2 CONT # ([1374]) DILSHAD Aga, daughter of SHAMS ed-Din & his wife Bujan Aga (-1383). 
2 CONT #([1377]) TUMAN Aga, daughter of Emir MUSA & his wife Arzu Mulk Aga ([1366]-). 
2 CONT # CHULPAN MULC Aga, daughter of HAJIBEY of Geté. 
2 CONT # ([1395]).  ---, daughter of KEZER KOJA Aglen.  
2 CONT # seven other wives/concubines … Ruy Gonzalez de Clavijo names "Dileoltagna, Cholpamalaga, Mundagasa, Vengarage, Ropa-arbaraga, and Yaugurage which means queen of the heart, and Timour Beg gave her that name last August" as the ot
2 CONC her wives of Timur[396].  
