2 DATE 4 NOV 1411
2 PLAC Rey
2 ADDR
3 CTRY Iran
1 OCCU
1 TITL Sultan
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} *https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Khalil_Sultan
2 CONT
2 CONT -------
2 CONT
2 CONT ref An Oriental Biographical Dictionary, Thomas William Beale. ... Also called Mirza Khalil and Khalil-ullah, was the son of Miranshah and grandson of Amir Timur, at whose death he, being present with the army at Samarqand, took p
2 CONC ossession of the country. This prince who was a person of excellent temper and had many good qualities, might have preserved the power he had acquired, had not his violent love for Shad-ul-Mulk, a celebrated coutezan, whom he had 
2 CONC secretly married, diverted him from the cares of the government. He had scarcely reigned four years, when he was seized by the chiefs who had raised him to the throne, and sent prisoner to the country of Kashghar in 1408 (811), wh
2 CONC ere instead of endeavouring to effect his release and recover his power, he spent the whole of his time in writing verses to his beloved mistress, who had been exposed, by the reverse of his fortune, to the most cruel indignities.
2 CONC  He was at last released by Mirza Shahrukh, his uncle, who had taken possession of his kingdom and who not only gave him the government of Rei, Kum and Hamadan, but restored his beautiful mistress to his arms. After this, he lived
2 CONC  two years and a half and died  on 6th of November 1411, aged 28 years and Shad ul-Mulk, on the occurrence of this event, acted a part which has given fame to her memory - she struck a poniard to her breast: and  the lovers were b
2 CONC uried in one tomb in the city of Rei. 
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT After Teimur's (Tamerlan's) death, his vast territories were divided between his sons and grand sons. The first of these was soltan Khalil, the son of Miran Shah. First, he ruled over Transoxiana. Then he was selected as the gover
2 CONC nor general of Iraq, by Shahrokh, Teimur's son, and ruled there for the rest of his life (1435 AD).
2 CONT
2 CONT ref: page 198 of The Book of Iran. Iranian History at a Glance by Reza Shabani
