1 NAME Niyaz /Moghadam - Javanshir/
2 GIVN Niyaz
2 _MARNM Moghadam - Javanshir
1 SEX M
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} When Shah Abbas I handed Maragheh over to the family, the Moghadam was not yet a massive tribe; it was initially just a tight-knit clan of 70 families.
2 CONT The Shah heavily financed and back-stopped Aqa and Niyaz. Using this royal backing, the brothers spent the 1610s and 1620s absorbing smaller splinter clans from the Otuziki and Javanshir confederations, as well as integrating loca
2 CONC l elements. 
2 CONT Contemporary records show that under their direct management, the tribe exploded from those 70 families into an army of 10,000 families. This turned them from a simple occupation force into the dominant ethnic group in Maragheh.2.
2 CONC  Aqa (Aqa Khan II): The Governor 
2 CONT Upon their father’s passing in 1625, Aqa took the dynastic mantle as Aqa Khan 
2 CONT The Urban Administrator: While his father was a nomad warrior, Aqa Khan II focused on settling the tribe into Maragheh's urban and agricultural landscape. He formalised the aqta' (land grants) for his military captains.The Safavid
2 CONC  Firewall: Ruling through the remainder of Shah Abbas I's reign and into the era of Shah Safi, Aqa Khan II kept Maragheh loyal to Isfahan (the Safavid capital). 
2 CONT When the Ottomans periodically poked at the borders of Azerbaijan, Aqa Khan II’s mobilised Moghadam horsemen served as the frontline defense force.
2 CONT
2 CONT Niyaz: The Co-Ruler and Brother
2 CONT As established in the original Safavid court histories by Fazli Isfahani Khuzani, Niyaz was indeed the biological brother of Mirza Hossein (Aqa Khan II).
2 CONT •	While Mirza Hossein handled the sedentary governorship and imperial politics of the Maragheh city center, Niyaz managed the vast nomadic cavalry wings and migratory pasture routes of the 10,000 families.
2 CONT •	This is why the snippet highlights them ruling the Otuziki and Javanshir splinters "together as brothers."
2 CONT
2 CONT The Tribal Commander: While Aqa handled the governance and royal court relations, his brother Niyaz took charge of the domestic, inter-tribal operations.
2 CONT Managing the Steppe: In Turkic tribal systems, one brother often managed the "civil" seat (the city/governorate) while the other managed the "military/nomadic" wings. Niyaz was the chief organiser of the tribe's massive pastures, 
2 CONC handling internal migration, water rights, and cavalry training.
2 CONT The Bloodline Anchor: Because the family reused names, many later branches of the Moghadam family tree who did not inherit the supreme governorship of the town instead traced their lineage directly back to the prestigious military
2 CONC  house of Niyaz
2 CONT How They Setup the Later "Khans" : By splitting the responsibilities, Aqa II and Niyaz ensured that the Moghadam did not dissolve into a civil war when their father died. They created a powerful, dual-system of tribal loyalty and 
2 CONC imperial recognition. This exact infrastructure allowed the family to survive the chaotic transition from the Safavids to Nader Shah, ultimately positioning later descendants like Ali Khan Moghadam and Haj Ali Mohammad Khan to rul
2 CONC e Maragheh as an autonomous Khanate.
