1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://ru.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D0%91%D1%83%D0%BB%D0%B0%D0%BA_(%D1%85%D0%B0%D0%BD_%D0%97%D0%BE%D0%BB%D0%BE%D1%82%D0%BE%D0%B9_%D0%9E%D1%80%D0%B4%D1%8B)
2 CONT
2 CONT Bulak (c. 1360 - 1380 , Muhammad Bulak , Giyas-ad-din Muhammad-khan , Muhammad-khan , Giyas-ad-din Bulyak-khan , Tat. Möxämmät Bulaq, Mөhәmmәt Bulak ) - Khan of Ulus Jochi (Golden Horde) in 1370-1372 and 1375, Khan of the Mamaev H
2 CONC orde (1370-1380)
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography 
2 CONT He was planted in childhood on the Khan's throne of Ulus Jochi (Golden Horde) by Beklyarbek Mamai after the death of his father, Khan Abdullah . Under Muhammad Bulak Khan, coins were minted in Hadji Tarkhan , Crimea and Majar .
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1372, the capital Saray was captured by the ruler of the eastern part of the Ulus Jochi (Golden Horde) Urus Khan , and Mohammed Bulak fled to Mamai in the Crimea. After the departure of Urus Khan to the east in 1374, Sarai capt
2 CONC ured Cherkess and was proclaimed Khan of the Ulus Jochi (Golden Horde). However, the Circassian Khan did not rule for long - at the beginning of 1375, Mamai captured Saray and returned the Khan's throne to Mohammed Bulak.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soon Urus Khan returned from the east and captured Saray for a month, but he could not gain a foothold - Mohammed Bulak again captured the capital and returned the Khan's throne.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the end of 1375, the capital Sarai was captured by Kaganbek Khan . Muhammad Bulak was no longer able to regain the khan's throne before his death in 1380, and probably died in the Battle of Kulikovo .
2 CONT
2 CONT Literature 
2 CONT Grekov B. D. , Yakubovsky A. Yu. The Golden Horde and its fall . - M. - L. , 1950. [1]
