2 CONT His father is Chidur Beg son of Uzbek son of Toghril Shah.
2 CONT
2 CONT Togrilcha († 1291)
2 CONT his sons :
2 CONT
2 CONT - Uzbek Khan (1282–1341), Khan of the Golden Horde 1312–1342
2 CONT   his sons   1  .Timur († 1328)
2 CONT                   2  .Tini Beg , Khan of the Golden Horde 1342
2 CONT                   3  .Dschani Beg († 1357), Khan of the Golden Horde 1342-1357
2 CONT                   4. Chidur Beg
2 CONT
2 CONT Dschani Beg's sons :
2 CONT                   1.Berdi Beg , Khan of the Golden Horde 1357–1359
2 CONT                   2.Qulpa, Khan in the Golden Horde 1359-1360
2 CONT                   3.Newruz Beg Mehmed, Khan in the Golden Horde 1359-1360
2 CONT
2 CONT Chidur Beg's son
2 CONT       .Abdallah, Khan in the Golden Horde 1361-1369 (candidate Mamais )
2 CONT
2 CONT https://de.zxc.wiki/wiki/Stammliste_der_Nachfahren_Dschingis_Khans
2 CONT ===
2 CONT Mamai came from the Qyjat tribe and was involved in these power struggles from 1361 onwards. In that year he defeated and killed Temur Hoga, the son of Hizyr Khan Mahmud; Hizyr was the 4th successor of Jani Beg and a Scheibanide .
2 CONC  In his place he installed his own khan named Abd Ullah (Russian Avdulja), a descendant of Uzbek , while the emirs in the Volga region appointed a khan named Mürid (Murat, Amurat, unclear origin).
2 CONT
2 CONT In the subsequent power struggle, however, neither side could prevail, Mamai won one battle for Abd Ullah and lost another. The khan made himself so hated by his murderous acts that Mürid's successor Azis (Osis, unknown origin, ru
2 CONC led 1364-1367 - murdered) was able to drive him to the Syr-darja in 1366/67 . Abd Ullah's coins existed until 1369.
2 CONT ref:https://de.zxc.wiki/wiki/Mamai
