1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Abdal-Latif Mirza,(c. 1420 – May 9, 1450) was the great-grandson of Central Asian emperor Timur. He was the third son of Ulugh Beg, Timurid ruler of Transoxiana (modern Uzbekistan, Tajikistan and parts of Turkmenis
2 CONC tan, Kyrgyzstan and Kazakhstan).
2 CONT
2 CONT Having been given the governorship of Balkh, Abdal-Latif Mirza served under his father. During the succession struggle that followed the death of Shah Rukh, he occupied Herat, although after Ulugh Beg left the city at the end of 1
2 CONC 448 it was conquered by Mirza Abul-Qasim Babur bin Baysonqor.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdal-Latif Mirza did not remain loyal to his father. Angry over the fact that he was to be passed over in the transfer of rule of Samarkand, he revolted while Ulugh Beg was marching to retake Khorasan. He defeated his father at D
2 CONC imashq, near Samarkand, in the fall of 1449.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ulugh Beg later decided to surrender himself, and Abdal-Latif Mirza granted him permission to take a pilgrimage to Mecca, but while Ulugh Beg was on his way he was ordered murdered by his son.
2 CONT
2 CONT This earned Abdal-Latif Mirza the infamous nickname "Padarkush" (from Persian "killer of his father، پدر کش"). A few days later he also had his brother 'Abd al-'Aziz killed.
2 CONT
2 CONT In this manner he became ruler of Transoxiana. A somewhat pious person, he gained the support of the local religious groups, but this did not save him from a conspiracy hatched against him by the amirs. His reign lasted for only s
2 CONC ix months. He was succeeded by his cousin Abdallah Mirza.
