1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Succeded by Sultan Hossein Baiqra surnamed Abul-Ghazi. a descendant of Amir Timur.https://www.geni.com/people/Bayqara-Mirza-I-petit-fils-de-Amir-Timur-Tamerlan-Teimurid-Gov-de-Perse-en-1394/6000000039886558171
2 CONT
2 CONT An Oriental Biographical Dictionary, Founded on Materials Collected by the Late Thomas William Beale mentions that he was slain by Uzzun Hassan in 1449.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abū Saʿīd Mirza (Chagatay/Persian: ابو سعید میرزا‎‎) was the son of Muḥammad son of Miran Shah son of Amir Timur (Herat, 1424–1469), and was a Timurid Empire ruler in Transoxiana, Khurasan and the southern Caspian region, what is 
2 CONC today parts of Turkmenistan, Kazakhstan, Uzbekistan, Iran, Azerbaijan and Afghanistan and member of the Timurid dynasty.
2 CONT
2 CONT Emperor of the Timurid Empire in what is now Kazakhstan, Uzbekistan, eastern Iran and Afghanistan.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abū Saʿīd was the great-grandson of Timur, the grandson of Miran Shah, and the nephew of Ulugh Beg. He was the grandfather of Babur, by his son Umar Sheikh Mirza, the founder of the Mughal Empire in South Asia. As a young man his 
2 CONC ancestry made him a principal in the century-long struggle for the remnants of Timur's empire waged between Timur's descendants, the Black Sheep Turkomans, and the White Sheep Turkomans (1405–1510).
2 CONT
2 CONT He raised an army but failed to gain a foothold in Samarkand or Bukhara (1448–1449); established his base at Yasi and conquered much of Turkestan in 1450. In June 1451, he captured Samarkand with the aid of the Uzbek Turks under A
2 CONC būl-Khayr Khān, thus securing rulership of the eastern part of Timur's Empire, Transoxiana. 
2 CONT
2 CONT He fought an inconclusive war with Mirza Abul-Qasim Babur bin Baysonqor of Khorasan in 1454; and took advantage of his cousin Jahan Shah's capture of Herat late in 1457 to capture it for himself in 1458, thus acquiring the rest of
2 CONC  Timur's heartland and becoming the most powerful of the Timurid princes in central Asia. He defeated an alliance of three other Timurid princes at the Battle of Sarakhs in March 1459, and conquered eastern Iran and most of Afghan
2 CONC istan by 1461, agreeing with Jahan Shah to divide Iran between them; when the White Sheep Turkoman chieftain Uzun Hasan attacked and killed Jahan Shah, Abu Sa'id spurned Uzun Hasan's peace offer and answered Jahan Shah's son's req
2 CONC uest for aid.
2 CONT
2 CONT Captured (on 11 February 1469) by Uzun Hasan with a small force at the calamitous Battle of Qarabagh (in modern Republic of Azerbaijan) during a campaign against the Ak Koyunlu (White Sheep) Turkomans,He was handed over to Yadgar 
2 CONC Muhammad Mirza on 17 February 1469, who executed him, ostensibly in retribution for Abū Sa'id's execution of Yadgar Muhammad Mirza's grandmother Queen Gawhar Shad, who had been intriguing against him.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abu Sa'id formed many political connections during his rule, including to the Uzbeks, the Qara Qoyunlu Turkmen, and a variety of Sufi figures. He has been linked to Khwāja ʿUbaydullah Aḥrār (d. 895 AH/1490 CE) at the time of his a
2 CONC ccession (1451 CE) in Samarqand. He also sought support at the shrine of Ahmad Yasavi in Yasi.
2 CONT
2 CONT Much has been made of Abū Saʿīd’s removal of tamgha taxes at the request of Khwāja Aḥrār, but these taxes were removed by earlier Timurids as well, and the action may have been as much a reversal of an earlier, little-liked ruler 
2 CONC as a shift in policy toward Islamic taxation systems. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Khwandamir reports that court officials were deposed and sometimes killed for misappropriating funds, including the same Quṭb al-Dīn Ṭaʾūs Simnanī who later is responsible for the construction of the Juy-i Sulṭānī. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Women could also be caught up in the court intrigues. As mentioned in Aubin and discussed more fully by Manz, Abū Saʿīd’s execution of Gawhar Shād, the wife of Shāh Rukh b. Timur (779-850/1377-1447) was viewed negatively by the co
2 CONC ntemporary chroniclers.
2 CONT
2 CONT Abū Saʿīd does not seem to have personally engaged in large-scale building projects, perhaps because of the time he spent on campaigns. Quṭb al-Dīn Ṭāʾūs, a vizier under Abū Saʿīd, built the Jūy-i Sulṭāni canal in Herat, which cre
2 CONC ated the possibility for future building under Sulṭān Ḥusayn Bayqārā as it was completed while Abū Saʿīd was on his final campaign to Azerbaijan. This and the Aq Sarāy (white palace) shifted the elite living space while the ruler 
2 CONC was in Herat to outside the city walls, “marking a conscious break with the past.”  Other public works credited to Abū Saʿīd include repairs to the Gulistān dam “while at the same time appropriating the lands it watered”.Buildings
2 CONC  include an aiwan at the musalla in Herat, repairs to Ghār-i Karukh which includes an inscription, and construction of a spa and bath at Ūba (Obeh), a “resort for the Timurids” in their summer quarters
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Uzun Hasan or Hassan (1423 – January 6, 1478) (Azerbaijani: اوزون حسن , Uzun Həsən; Turkish: Uzun Hasan, where uzun means "tall"; Persian: اوزون حسن‎‎) was a sultan of the Aq Qoyunlu dynasty, also known as the White Sheep Turkoman
2 CONC s.[3] Hassan was ruler of Azerbaijan which included also parts of present-day Iraq, Turkey, Azerbaijan, Iran and Syria between 1453 and 1478.
2 CONT
2 CONT Timur appointed his great-grandfather, Kara Yülük Osman, as a governor of Diyarbakır, with the cities of Erzincan, Mardin, Ruha (or Urfa), and Sivas. Later, Azerbaijan was divided between two Timurid rulers, Jahan Shah of Kara Koy
2 CONC unlu (the Black Sheep Turkoman) and Uzun Hasan. After twenty years of fighting, Uzun Hasan eventually defeated Jahan Shah in a battle near the sanjak of Çapakçur[4][5] in present day eastern Turkey on October 30[6] (or November 11
2 CONC [7]), 1467. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Upon the defeat of the latter, another Timurid ruler, Abu Sa'id Mirza, answered Jahan Shah's son's request for aid, taking much of Jahan Shah's former land and going to war with Uzun Hasan despite the latter's offers of peace. Uzu
2 CONC n Hasan then ambushed and captured Abu Sa'id at the Battle of Qarabagh, whereupon he was executed by Yadgar Muhammad Mirza, a rival.
2 CONT
2 CONT [...On 26 Šaʿbān 861/19 July 1457, the Timurid ruler Sultan Abu Saʿid conquered Herat. He treated Gowhar-šād well, but when he was informed that she was sharing information with Ebrāhim b. ʿAlāʾ-al-Dowla, and that the powerful Šir
2 CONC  Ḥāji, fearing for his own life as the murderer of Tarḵān amirs, would not serve him while Gowhar-šād was alive, he had her executed on 9 Ramażān 861/31 July 1457 (Maṭlaʿ-e saʿdayn, ed. Šafiʿ, II/2, pp. 1143-44; Ḥabib al-siar, Teh
2 CONC ran, IV, pp. 67-69).] [...Except for Dawlatšāh Samarqandi, Timurid historians mention Gowhar-šād with respect and condemn her execution. On 22 Rajab 873/5 February 1469, Uzun Ḥasan Āq Qoyunlu handed over Sultan Abu Saʿid to Gowhar
2 CONC -šād’s great-grandson, Yādgār Moḥammad, who killed him (Abu Bakr Ṭehrāni, p. 491).] ref:http://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/gowhar-sad-aga
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Abu_Sa%27id_Mirza
