1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://fmg.ac/Projects/MedLands/RUSSIA,%20Rurik.htm#_Toc198014312
2 CONT
2 CONT FEDOR Nikitich Godunov, son of --- (-[1568/70]). 
2 CONT
2 CONT m ---.  The name of Fedor´s wife is not known. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Fedor & his wife had two children: 
2 CONT
2 CONT 1.             BORIS Fedorevich Godunov (1552-13./23 Apr 1605).  He dominated the reign of his brother-in-law Tsar Fedor I and took the title koniushii/master of the horse.  After the death of Tsar Fedor, Boris Godunov engineered 
2 CONC his own accession and succeeded as BORIS Tsar of all Russias 17 Feb 1598, crowned 3 Sep 1598.  The dynastic uncertainty of this succession, added to existing social and other problems faced by Russia, plunged the country into the 
2 CONC "Time of Troubles" during which the authority of the central government declined drastically.  m MARIA Grigorievna, daughter of MALYUTA Skuratov-Belsky & his wife --- (-murdered Moscow 10 Jun 1605).  Boris & his wife had two child
2 CONC ren: 
2 CONT
2 CONT a)             FEDOR Borisovich (1589-murdered Moscow 10 Jun 1605).  He succeeded his father as FEDOR III Tsar of all Russias from 13 Apr 1605 to 1 Jun 1605.   
2 CONT
2 CONT b)             XENIA Borisovna Godunova (1582-1622).  Her marriage was arranged by her father in an attempt to boost his international prestige when he faced mounting domestic problems, but the bridegroom died very soon after the 
2 CONC marriage.  m (1602) JOHAN Prince of Denmark, son of FREDERIK II King of Denmark & his wife Sophie von Mecklenburg (Haderslebeb 26 Jul 1583-Moscow 28 Oct 1602, bur Moscow German Church, transferred 1637 to Roskilde Cathedral). 
2 CONT
2 CONT 2.             IRINA Fedorovna Godunova (1557-25 Oct 1603).  After the death of her husband, some consideration was given to declaring her his successor but she eventually became a nun[1101].  m (1580) FEDOR Ivanovich of Russia, s
2 CONC on of IVAN IV "Grozhny/the Terrible" Tsar of all Russias & his first wife Anastasia Romanovna Zakharina (31 May 1557-6 Jan 1598).  He succeeded his father in 1584 as FEDOR I Tsar of all Russias.
