1 NAME Haj Ali Mohammad Khan /Moghadam (r. 1750s)/
2 GIVN Haj Ali Mohammad Khan
2 _MARNM Moghadam (r. 1750s)
1 SEX M
1 OCCU
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Following his father’s successful military campaigns, Haj Ali Mohammad Khan Moghadam took over the leadership of the Maragheh Khanate.
2 CONT His administration represents the bridge between the chaotic independent era of the mid-18th century and the official dawn of the Qajar Dynasty.
2 CONT 1. Securing the Borders of the Khanate
2 CONT When Haj Ali Mohammad Khan came to power, the region of Azerbaijan was a battlefield for rival khans.
2 CONT •	He focused on fortifying Maragheh against aggressive neighboring factions, most notably the expansionist Khans of Urmia (the Afshars) and the Khans of Tabriz (the Donboli family).
2 CONT •	Instead of over-extending his army in foreign conquests like his father, he prioritized domestic stability. He turned Maragheh into a heavily defended agricultural stronghold.
2 CONT 2. The Internal Struggle with the Afshars
2 CONT Though the Moghadams had largely displaced the Afshar tribe from the city administration decades prior, local Afshar pockets still frequently challenged Moghadam authority. Haj Ali Mohammad Khan launched a series of small-scale lo
2 CONC cal operations to finally subdue these remaining rival clans. He forced them to pay tribute to his court, solidifying the Moghadams as the absolute, undisputed masters of the region.
2 CONT 3. Diplomatic Realignment with the Rising Qajars
2 CONT As the Zand Dynasty began to decline in the late 18th century, a new power was rising in northern Iran: Agha Mohammad Khan Qajar (the brutal founder of the Qajar Dynasty).
2 CONT •	Haj Ali Mohammad Khan recognized that resisting the Qajars would mean the complete destruction of Maragheh.
2 CONT •	He initiated diplomatic talks with the Qajar camp, offering political alignment and military access through the strategic Maragheh passes in exchange for a guarantee that his family would keep their hereditary rights.
2 CONT 4. Passing the Torch to Ahmed Khan
2 CONT Haj Ali Mohammad Khan’s peaceful, calculated diplomacy set up his son—the legendary Ahmed Khan Moghadam—for unprecedented greatness. Because the family had aligned with the Qajars early, Ahmed Khan did not just remain Khan of Mara
2 CONC gheh; he was promoted by the new Qajar Shahs to become the Beglerbegi (Governor-General) of both Tabriz and Azerbaijan.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Haj Ali Mohammad Khan Moghadam was the son and immediate successor of Aliqoli Khan at the head of the Maragheh Khanate.
2 CONT As established by the traditional succession rules of the Moghadam dynasty, the lineage moved directly from father to son during this mid-to-late 18th-century transition:
2 CONT •	The Transition: When Aliqoli Khan was summoned to Shiraz to live as a high-ranking political hostage under Karim Khan Zand, Haj Ali Mohammad Khan took over the active day-to-day administrative governance back home in Maragheh.
2 CONT •	The Succession: Upon his father's eventual passing, he fully assumed the hereditary mantle as the undisputed Khan of Maragheh and supreme leader of the Moghadam tribe.
2 CONT He successfully navigated the family through the rest of the Zand era before passing the leadership down to his own son, the legendary Qajar-era General Ahmed Khan Moghadam.
