1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [Amir Khan Mawsihi, regent at Hirat on behalf of Prince Tahmasp, tried to benefit from the afflictions of Muhammad Zaman Mirza in Gharjistan (end of 923/beginning of 1518). Weakened by these new foes B , Muhammad Z
2 CONC aman Mirza could not resist the attack of Ibrahim Jaluq, into whose hands he had the misfortune to fall. Conveyed a prisoner from Balkh to Kabul, Muhammad Zaman Mirza. returned to Balkh as the governor, for he had repaired his for
2 CONC tune by marrying at Kabul the daughter of Babur ] ref page 87 of https://archive.org/stream/HistoryOfShahIsmailSafawi-GhulamSarwar/119900259-History-of-Shah-Isma-il-Safawi-Ghulam-Sarwar_djvu.txt
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Masuma_Sultan_Begum_(daughter_of_Babur)
2 CONT
2 CONT Masuma Sultan Begum (Persian: معصومہ سلطان بیگم‎; born c. 1508) was a Mughal princess and the daughter of the first Mughal emperor, Babur. She is frequently mentioned in the Humayun-nama by her sister, Gulbadan Begum, who calls he
2 CONC r sister 'Elder sister Moon' (mah chacha).[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Early life Masuma Sultan Begum was the daughter of Babur and his fourth wife, Masuma Sultan Begum.[2] She was born in Kabul, and her mother died giving birth to her. She was given her mother's name.[3] In 1511, Babur entrusted Kab
2 CONC ul to his younger brother Nasir Mirza and set out for Samarkand.
2 CONT
2 CONT Marriage In 1517, when Masuma Sultan Begum was nine years old, Babur married her to the twenty-one year old Muhammad Zaman Mirza.[4] He was the son of Badi' al-Zaman Mirza, and the grandson of Sultan Husayn Mirza Bayqara.[5] His m
2 CONC other was the daughter of Tahamtan Beg, and the niece of Asad Beg.[6] After Masuma Sultan Begum's marriage with him, Babur sent him to Balkh.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT She became a widow at the age of thirty-one when Muhammad Zaman Mirza died in the Battle of Chausa.[7]
