1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Ulug Mehmed seized the throne immediately after the overthrow of the Emir Edigü and the mutual annihilation of several sons of Toktamish (1419 coin from Astrakhan) and sent envoys to the Timurid ruler Shah Rukh (14
2 CONC 21). According to Schiltberger , he is said to have seized the government in the fight against Edigü and his protégé Chekre Khan.
2 CONT
2 CONT The khan constantly struggled with rivals. One of them was Devlet Berdi , who was able to establish himself in the Crimea after 1424 and founded the Crimean Khanate there. In 1424 Ulug Mehmed was defeated by the Siberian Khan Bora
2 CONC q (1422-28) and lost the throne for about three years. He fled with his son to Grand Duke Witold at the court of the Grand Duchy of Lithuania . However, Boraq showed no interest in the cities of the Horde - there are no coins - an
2 CONC d instead oriented itself towards the prosperous Syr-Darya area and the Timurid Empire . In his place, Devlet Berdi seized power, sent an ambassador to Egypt in March 1427 and gave a. a. 1427/28 coins out in New Sarai and Astrakha
2 CONC n. In 1428 Ulug Mehmed finally regained the throne by killing Boraq - a merit that is also attributed to Boraq's Siberian rival, the Uzbek Khan Abu'l-Chair (r. 1428–1468). https://de.zxc.wiki/wiki/Ulug_Mehmed
2 CONT ====
2 CONT
2 CONT Dawlat Berdi (died 1432), also known as Devlet Berdi, was a Khan of the Golden Horde who reigned from 1419 to 1421, and again from 1428 to his death in 1432. He was the son of Jabbar Berdi and a descendant of Berke Khan.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first reign was brief, lasting from 1419 to 1421, when he and his rival Ulugh Muhammad were defeated by Baraq.[1] After Baraq's assassination in 1427, Dawlat established himself in Crimea. Ulugh Muhammad attempted an invasion 
2 CONC of his territory in 1430, but was unable to defeat Berdi and retreated following the death of Vytautas, his main supporter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Due to the efforts of Hacı I Giray Dawlat was never able to consolidate control over Crimea[2] and was assassinated in 1432. His son, Äxmät, proved unable to resist the combined forces of Ulugh Muhammad and the Crimean Tatars and 
2 CONC was defeated the following year, leading to the creation of the Crimean Khanate.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT An unnamed daughter may have become the wife of John IV of Trebizond.[4]
2 CONT
2 CONT References
2 CONT
2 CONT     Paine, Sheila: The Golden Horde: From the Himalaya to the Mediterranean, Penguin Books, 1998.
2 CONT
2 CONT , Bosworth, Clifford Edmund, The New Islamic Dynasties: A Chronological and Genealogical Manual, p. 253. Edinburgh University Press, 2004.
2 CONT Hostler, Charles Warren: The Turks of Central Asia, p. 30. Praeger Publishers, 1993.
2 CONT Hostler, Charles Warren: The Turks of Central Asia, p. 33. Praeger Publishers, 1993. Profile of Alexios IV and his children in "Medieval Lands" by Charles Cawley
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Dawlat Berdi (died 1432), also known as Devlet Berdi, was a Khan of the Golden Horde who reigned from 1419 to 1421, and again from 1428 to his death in 1432. He was the son of Jabbar Berdi and a descendant of Berke Khan.
2 CONT
2 CONT His first reign was brief, lasting from 1419 to 1421, when he and his rival Ulugh Muhammad were defeated by Baraq.[1] After Baraq's assassination in 1427, Dawlat established himself in Crimea. Ulugh Muhammad attempted an invasion 
2 CONC of his territory in 1430, but was unable to defeat Berdi and retreated following the death of Vytautas, his main supporter.
2 CONT
2 CONT Due to the efforts of Hacı I Giray Dawlat was never able to consolidate control over Crimea[2] and was assassinated in 1432. His son, Äxmät, proved unable to resist the combined forces of Ulugh Muhammad and the Crimean Tatars and 
2 CONC was defeated the following year, leading to the creation of the Crimean Khanate.[3]
2 CONT
2 CONT An unnamed daughter may have become the wife of John IV of Trebizond.[4]
