2 CONT Mirza Mohammad-Hossein Khan Moqaddam Maraqei (Ajudan Bashi)
2 CONT
2 CONT From Wikipedia, the free encyclopedia
2 CONT
2 CONT Abdol Hossein Khan (1839 - Ambassadeur aupres de l'Angleterre) contemporain de Jafar Moshir Dowleh - Moghadam Maraghei
2 CONT
2 CONT 1866 (killed in the battle of Ganja) 
2 CONT He died 1866-67. He left a son Ali Khan, who died in 1884[4][5] Hossein Pasha Mir Panj - Mirza Hossein Khan; Il est le 5eme gouverneur du Maragheh Khanate, apres Aliqulu xan Muqqadam Haji Alimohammad Khan Muqaddam Ahmad Khan Muqad
2 CONC dam Jafarqulu xan Muqaddam Mirza Hossein Khan apres lui viennent Haj Ali Khan (1807-1867) aka Etemad Saltaneh I Haj Ali Khan Hajeb ol Dowleh Mohammad Hassan Khan Muqaddam (1843-1896) Samed Khan Shucaeddvie (1852-1914) Iskender Kha
2 CONC n Sardar Nasser Muqaddam (ref: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Maragheh_Khanate)
2 CONT
2 CONT Mirza Mohammad-Hossein Khan Moqaddam Maraqei (Ajudan Bashi) Mirza Hossein Khan Ajudan Bashi was a 19th-century Iranian diplomat. He was a Member of the Maragheh Khanate. He was sent to France in 1839 by the Persian ruler Mohammad 
2 CONC Shah Qajar following the conflict between Persia and Great Britain over the possession of Herat in Afghanistan. In the wake of the Herat affair, Great Britain would remove its military and diplomatic missions from Persia, and occu
2 CONC py Kharg island and attack Bushehr.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT By dispatching Mirza Hossein Khan, Mohammad Shah Qajar resumed diplomatic relations with France. The diplomatic mission reached Louis-Philippe and asked for military help. In response, a group of French officers was sent to Persia
2 CONC  with the returning ambassador.[2] Louis-Philippe said:
2 CONT
2 CONT "God is my witness that we are pleased to receive [the Shah's] friendly greetings and we wish with all our heart, and throughout our life, to enjoy friendship and sincerity in our relations with the Shah".
2 CONT
2 CONT — Louis-Philippe.[3] Mirza Hossein Khan also visited Vienna and London as Ambassador Extraordinary, where Palmerston refused to receive him and rejected his mission. He died 1866-67. He left a son Ali Khan, who died in 1884
2 CONT
