2 CONC en Persia and Great Britain over the possession of Herat in Afghanistan. In the wake of the Herat affair, Great Britain would remove its military and diplomatic missions from Persia, and occupy Kharg island and attack Bushehr.[1] 
2 CONC By dispatching Mirza Hossein Khan, Mohammad Shah Qajar resumed diplomatic relations with France. The diplomatic mission reached Louis-Philippe and asked for military help. In response, a group of French officers was sent to Persia
2 CONC  with the returning ambassador.[2] Louis-Philippe said: "God is my witness that we are pleased to receive [the Shah's] friendly greetings and we wish with all our heart, and throughout our life, to enjoy friendship and sincerity i
2 CONC n our relations with the Shah". — Louis-Philippe Mirza Hossein Khan also visited Vienna and London as Ambassador Extraordinary, where Palmerston refused to receive him and rejected his mission. He died 1866-67. He left a son Ali K
2 CONC han, who died in 1884. Hossein Pacha Agha Adjudan Bashi Mir Panj Moghadam 5eme Khan de Maragheh Gouverneur de Maragheh sous Mohammad Shah Qajar et Nassereddin Shah Colonel (سرهنگ) ĀJŪDĀN-BĀŠĪ a Persian term translating the French 
2 CONC military title adjudant-en-chef; aide and deputy to the army commander during the Qajar period. Hossein Khan Moghadam est soit le fils de Ahmad Khan Moghadam (Agha Khan), emir réputé de l'époque des Safavi, soit son neveu. Il etai
2 CONC t entre dans le corps militaire des sa jeunesse.
