1 NOTE {geni:about_me} [http://www.almanachdegotha.org/id221.html The 16th century Lithuanian-Polish Nobility]
2 CONT =Kristupas Radvila=
2 CONT Born: 22 March 1585; Biržai, Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth;
2 CONT
2 CONT Parents:
2 CONT
2 CONT * Krzysztof Mikołaj "the Thunderbolt" Radziwiłł;
2 CONT * Katarzyna Ostrogska;
2 CONT
2 CONT Died: 19 November 1640 (aged 55), Świadość, Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth; Religion:	Calvinist;
2 CONT
2 CONT Spouse: Anna Kiszka
2 CONT Children (with Anna Kiszka):
2 CONT * Janusz Radziwiłł;
2 CONT * Katarzyna Radziwiłł;
2 CONT
2 CONT [https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Krzysztof_Radziwi%C5%82%C5%82 Prince Krzysztof Radziwiłł] (Christopher Radvila the Younger, Lithuanian: Kristupas Radvila) (22 March 1585, Biržai – 19 November 1640) was a Polish-Lithuanian noble (sz
2 CONC lachcic), and a notable magnate, politician, and military commander of his epoch. Sometimes referred to as Krzysztof Radziwiłł II, to distinguish him from his father, Krzysztof Mikołaj 'Piorun' (The Thunder) Radziwłł.
2 CONT
2 CONT A proud magnate, sensitive to the point of ancestral honor and heritage, convinced of his vocation to play a leading role in the country. He was a successful military leader, but selfishness and pride divided his supporters and we
2 CONC akened his political significance. As the leader of the Lithuanian Calvinists, he was often threatened and disliked by the zealous and ultra-Catholic King of Poland, Sigismund III Vasa.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Biography==
2 CONT
2 CONT Owner of Biržai, he was Field Hetman of Lithuania from 1615, Castellan of Vilnius from 1633, Voivode of Vilnius Voivodeship from 1633, Great Lithuanian Hetman from 1635, elder of Mahilyow, Bystrzyca, Žiežmariai, and Seje. In 1632,
2 CONC  he served as Marshal of the Sejm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kristupas Radvila successfully fought in Polish Livonia against Sweden from 1600 to 1617. He participated in the campaign against the Swedes in the Baltic region in 1621–1622 (Polish-Swedish War), where he agreed to a controversia
2 CONC l truce (as he had no permission to negotiate it from the king or the Sejm). An important commander of Lithuanian forces during the Smolensk War, where he was instrumental in obtaining the capitulation of the Russian army in the r
2 CONC elief of Smolensk. Afterwards, in 1635, he retired from his military career, concentrating on administering his estates. A Calvinist, he was a great protector of Protestants in Lithuania.
2 CONT
2 CONT Kristupas Radvila opposed the Catholic king Sigismund III Vasa, but supported his more tolerant son, Władysław IV. An advocate of Władysław's marriage to a Protestant princess, he distanced himself from the king after Władysław de
2 CONC clined this marriage proposal. He opposed an alliance between the Commonwealth and the Habsburgs. On his lands in Kėdainiai, he founded a major Calvinist cultural and religious center, which flourished till the 19th century as a c
2 CONC enter of the Polish Reformed Church. His large horse stables were famous throughout Europe.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Collector==
2 CONT
2 CONT Radziwiłł collected various curiosities at the Lubcha Castle: minerals, mollusk shells, corals, hunting trophies, whale bones, elephant tusks, nut shells, and similar items. It is believed that at least three items (a moose horn c
2 CONC ollection, a chair made of horns, and a jaw bone of a bowhead whale) of the collection are preserved at the Zoological Museum of Vilnius University.
